  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 146 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nLittle change in the synoptic pattern has occurred over the past 24 \nhours with mid-level ridging in place over the Plains and a closed \nlow slowly moving east into the southwest CONUS. Warm and dry \nconditions remain the rule across the forecast area with mostly \nsunny skies and highs near 80 degrees this afternoon. Low-level \nsoutherly flow increases tonight into Friday as a surface low \ndeepens on the lee of the Rockies. Good mixing and south winds \ngusting at 20-30 MPH will be offset to some degree by passing mid \nand high clouds, with the net result being another day of highs \naround 80 degrees. \n\nGuidance continues to slow the progression of the western trough, as \nnow the northern stream wave is progged to remain farther north \nwhich slows the southern stream trough and surface cold front. In \naddition, more ensemble members are lifting the southern stream \nenergy farther north, keeping the cold front north of the forecast \narea and highs above-normal through at least Monday. Confidence has \ndecreased in precipitation chances especially with eastern extent \ngiven the slower and more northerly track of the system. Central \nKansas has the highest chances (30-50%) at seeing some rain/storms \nSunday night through Tuesday morning. Still expect some cooler air \nto filter in behind the system, but exactly how cool remains \nuncertain. Large spreads persist (15 degrees between NBM 25th-75th \npercentiles) in potential high temperatures Tuesday into the middle \nof next week, but should be closer to climatological norms behind \nthe system.",
